  "summary": "Host David Chen explored the topic of artificial intelligence in healthcare, aiming to distinguish between the hype and the current reality of AI implementation in the sector. Guests Dr. Priya Patel and Alex Moore discussed the practical applications and significant limitations of AI in clinical settings. Dr. Patel confirmed that AI is being used in areas like reading X-rays and flagging urgent cases, emphasizing that these tools are assistive rather than replacements for clinicians. She also highlighted tangible patient-facing examples such as smart triage chatbots on hospital websites that help guide patients on next steps.\n\nAlex Moore pointed out major obstacles to widespread adoption, specifically mentioning the substantial regulatory barriers imposed by the FDA, which prevent full autonomy from being implemented soon. Furthermore, the discussion touched upon critical issues like data privacy and the potential for bias, as AI learns from existing clinical data which might be skewed. Despite these hurdles, Dr. Patel noted that hospitals are adopting AI primarily for efficiency and to provide clinician support by enabling faster reads and reducing errors. Alex Moore added that the acceleration of digital health during the pandemic also contributed to the increased adoption of these tools. Looking ahead, Dr. Patel suggested that future developments will involve AI copilots that suggest treatment plans, but stressed that a human will always retain the final decision-making authority, with interoperability being crucial for these systems to function effectively.\n"
